hello and welcome to the daily climate  show coming up  with much of europe in a severe heatwave  we report from portugal which is on a  high state of alert for wildfires  irreversible biodiversity damage could  kill off mankind a dire warning from the  environment agency chief but there is  hope  and how barbie is turning dr jane  goodall to a life in plastic ahead of  world chimpanzee day  [Music]  hello and welcome to the daily climate  show where we track the changes  happening to our world hear from those  hit hardest by those changes and report  on the race for solutions  so to start portugal is bracing itself  for record temperatures this week with  the mercury set to hit 46 or even 47  degrees in some regions the country is  now at the third highest of four alert  levels with the public banned from  accessing forests deemed to be at risk  there have been hundreds of wildfires in  recent days with thousands of  firefighters tackling the blazes but  forecasters are warning that the worst  is yet to come  much of europe is caught in a severe  heat wave temperatures in some parts of  france are expected to reach 39 degrees  this week heightening concerns they  could trigger further wildfires  the italian government has declared a  state of emergency in five northern  regions due to drought with crops  failing and the country's longest river  drying up parts of spain are expected to  reach 42 degrees this week in some areas  along the border with portugal  and in portugal itself the wildfires  have left at least 29 people injured as  thousands of firefighters and dozens of  aircraft there battle those blazes while  temperatures are forecast to reach  upwards of 45 degrees  from there our climate change  correspondent hannah thomas peter sent  this report  we're in an area north of lisbon where a  wildfire blazed through this area one of  the worst actually thousands of  firefighters battling hundreds of blazes  in extreme temperatures things are under  control relatively speaking for now but  let me show you why it's a problem  people live in these areas communities  have been fighting the fires away from  their homes and hoping that it doesn't  destroy  all they own this isn't just an issue  here in portugal it's an issue across  europe and climate change say scientists  is helping to drive the conditions that  make all of this worse increasingly  frequent increasingly severe heat events  and drought events combining in a toxic  combination really to extend fire  seasons and misery for millions of  people  now  the forecast is for more extreme  temperatures in the coming days heading  up to perhaps 46 degrees in this part of  europe but spain and france and of  course the uk going to be feeling those  temperatures later in the week too  climate change correspondent hannah  thomas peter there reporting from  portugal let's get some more climate  news now nearly 100 billion pieces of  plastic are thrown away each year by uk  households the greenpeace survey also  found just 12 percent of all plastic  waste is likely to be recycled in the uk  and more than 80 percent of it comes  from food and drink packaging it  highlights that plastic bottles and cups  can take  450 years to break down while releasing  chemicals into the soil  the uk installed the lowest number of  heat pumps in europe last year less than  43 000 were installed in 2021 according  to the european heat pump association  this falls far short of the target set  by the government to install 600 000  every year by 2028.  installing pumps reduces carbon  emissions produced through heating homes  by drawing heat from the air or the  ground  germany's vice chancellor and climate  minister says keeping the country's  remaining nuclear power reactors running  will do little to address the problems  caused by a potential shortfall in gas  supply robert habeck's comments come as  europe continues to struggle with an  energy crisis following a fall in supply  of russian gas damaging the continent's  energy security germany and the  netherlands have previously said they'll  reactivate coal power plants to make up  for the shortfall  now humanity is closer than ever to  irreversible biodiversity damage which  could kill humans that's the warning  from the environment agency chief today  in a dire speech to mark a report which  reveals the extent of biodiversity loss  across the uk sir james bevin explained  that people not just plants and animals  are at risk if we don't take better care  of the natural world  now you could say uh does this matter  does it really matter if the vole  goes the way of the dodo  well yes it does matter it matters  immensely because the biodiversity  crisis and let's be clear that's what it  is  it won't just kill the plants and the  animals it's killing it will kill us as  well  that's because nature provides us with a  host of things that we depend on  well the speech wasn't all doom and  gloom though so james did make it clear  that it's not too late to turn things  around if natural habitats are restored  well joining me now to talk more about  this is sarah fowler chief executive of  the wildfowl and wetlands trust who've  been working with the environment agency  on this sarah thank you so much for  joining us so could you explain a bit  about why we're losing so much  biodiversity and also what sort of life  forms are being particularly affected  so so nature is beautiful essential but  it is threatened  for us here at the wild final weapons  trust we're particularly concerned about  the alarming loss that we've seen in  wetland habitat and wetland species  we've lost almost 7 90  of wetlands globally since in the last  100 years  across across the nation in the world  now our wetlands are incredibly  important they're the lifeblood of the  planet and they're important service  stations for birds breeding birds um  like the curly like the blacktail godwit  and we're starting to see losses of  those and we're doing a lot of work  there but they're also really important  for natural toads damselflies those  beautiful species that you see and we're  seeing that across the across the piece  not just in fresh water but also in our  terrestrial habitat like trees and  woodlands  as well as being lost massively  and the study shows that 15 of flora and  fauna are at serious risk of extinction  what would happen if that 15 did become  extinct  the scary thing is we've lost species  already as a nation and as a globe  and these species are really important  now indicators for the health of the  habitat so as we start losing species  it's telling us a really important  signal that actually our habitat is in a  massive decline when our habitat that  the species live on is in a decline  we're losing the essential services that  the habitat provides  so wetlands are incredible carbon stores  they store carbon and prevent it going  into the atmosphere they're incredibly  good at helping us buffer against  extreme weather events we know about the  hot weather at the moment wetlands and  cities are incredibly cooling the  environment they're really good at  buffering us against us flood risks as  we lose species we lose those services  basically that our natural habitats  provide to us so it has a real personal  impact on us and our cities and then  every different individual in the nation  and are we losing these wetlands to  housing and industry and things like  that  if that's the case what needs to change  we need to stop building houses on on  wetlands  so we there's a range of reasons why  we're losing habitats we built on them  for wetlands we've drained them and  we're polluting them all of those things  have a massive impact on the habitats  and the resilience of our habitats what  we need to do is protect and protect  what we've already got let's keep hold  and not lose any more biodiversity or  any more wetland habitats or habitats at  all and then we need to restore and  create and that's where there's a huge  hope for us as a nation and as a planet  that if we start looking at solutions  that build in wetlands and build in  nature back into infrastructure we can  start building that natural service that  our habitats and nature provide to us we  can start building in flood defence  schemes that are natural flood defense  schemes we can start building in natural  ways of storing carbon in our salt  marshes  but just one really simple example for  you we've been working with the  environment agency to on a major coastal  realignment project in the seven estuary  called stiet  that was created in 2011  it's managed by ourselves at wwt it's a  much cheaper and sustainable solution  for protecting homes from flooding but  it also actually stores 18 000 tons of  carbon in the four years since it's been  working actively as a wetland habitat  and to put that into context that hope  that amount of carbon stored is as much  as if you planted one million trees and  have grown them for 10 years nature can  provide a brilliant solution to some of  the challenges we've got with climate  that's so brilliant to hear so just  quickly do you think enough is being  done to restore these habitats or do you  think the government perhaps needs to  invest more money  i i i'd like us to build a future  revolution that's both green and blue a  future revolution for infrastructure  building that builds in nature  designs in right from the start  i think more can be done on that  and we at wwt and other environmental  ngos stand ready to help that happen  wetlands and nature can be our solution  to the biodiversity crisis climate  triceps crisis and also the well-being  crisis as well  sarah fowler chief executive of the  wildfowl and wetlands trust thank you  very much for joining us today  now just before we leave you barbie have  announced a doll modelled on the  celebrated conservationist dr jane  goodall ahead of world chimpanzee day  the environmentalist is well known for  her work in protecting the primates from  extinction and she's hoping that the  dolls will encourage youngsters to study  nature  when i heard that  jane bobby doll was going to be made i  was really happy because actually i'd  been suggesting it for a long time that  girls don't want just to be film stars  and things like that but many of them  like me want to be out in nature  studying animals and so a barbie doll  whose jane is a super idea  and as you saw that doll comes with a  pair of binoculars and a notebook and a  chimpanzee definitely one for the  christmas lists now just before we go if  you've got any questions about the  climate issues in the show or literally  anything about climate change do send us  what's puzzling you and we'll do our  best to give you an answer on our  weekend climate show hosted by the  environment broadcaster and author tom  heap if you scan the qr code on your  screen right now you can send us those  questions directly that is all from us  today thanks so much for watching we'll  see you next time bye

